  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 PM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals mostly zonal flow aloft \nwith some weak embedded shortwave action skirting through the \nregion. Lingering moisture and northerly flow on the backside of a \ndeparting surface trough is keeping cloudy conditions overhead with \nareas of fog and drizzle present across the upslope locations of the \nwest and central. High temperatures have only managed to peak into \nthe 40s to low 50s for much of the northern tier of the UP while \nareas of the south-central climb into the mid to upper 50s. A few \nlight lake enhanced showers are possible tonight across the north \nand east where 850 mb temps cool between 1-3C. Otherwise, dry and \nseasonable weather holds through much of the mid-week under a period \nof mid-level ridging and surface high pressure. Temps tomorrow look \nvery similar to today though some clearing and dry surface air will \nallow overnight temps to fall into the 30s and 40s overnight.\n\nThe main concern of this forecast period continues to be the \nevolution of a surface low in the Great Lakes driven by two phasing \ntroughs Thursday night through Friday. Operational deterministic \nmodels and their ensemble counterparts are in pretty good agreement \nthat a ~1009 mb surface low will be placed in the vicinity of \nsouthern Lake Michigan by Thursday evening. Ample northward moisture \ntransport and isentropic ascent ahead of the lead northern stream \ntrough will provide enough support for widespread rain lifting into \nthe UP through Thursday afternoon through Friday morning and latest \nNBM suggests at least a 50-80% chance for QPF >0.25\". Early Friday \nmorning through Fri afternoon the surface low feature is slated to \nwork northeast atop the Lower Lakes, placing Upper Michigan on the \nnorthwestern flank. Strong mid to level cold air advection and rapid \npressure rises (upwards of 1.5-2mb/hr) will contribute to northerly \nwind speeds reaching 25-35 kts gusting to near 50 kts on Lake \nSuperior and the adjacent lakeshores. There is some uncertainty \nregarding the maximum gust potential as ensembles (NBM and ENS) have \nmoderate to high probability (50-70% chance) of gusts >50 kts \nwhereas model soundings reveal peak mixing largely near 45 kts. Lake \neffect/enhanced rain on the backside of the system will provide an \nadditional 0.1-0.25\" of precip once system rain clear the area. \nWouldn't surprise me to see some wet snow mix into areas of the \ninterior highlands early Friday morning before temperature warm into \nthe 40s Friday afternoon. No accumulations expected. As winds settle \nlate Friday and dry high pressure clears skies from west to east, \ntemperatures will drop like a rock overnight into Saturday morning, \nreaching the mid to low 20s for much if not all of the interior \nwhile the lakeshores may reach freezing for the first time this \nfall. Ridging and dry weather dominate through the rest of the \nweekend with the potential for another warm up / fire weather \npattern Sunday into Monday.",
